WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.789 --> 00:00:06.109
The zany relationship between the Venom symbiote
and Eddie Brock in Venom was fun to watch,

00:00:06.109 --> 00:00:10.260
but what everyone really wants to see is the
alien anti-hero take on Peter Parker.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:12.800
Or at least, that's what Sony Pictures thinks.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:17.560
And according to one of the movie's co-writers,
that very meet-up is in the cards right now.

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:22.660
In the lead-up to Venom's theatrical release
in October 2018, producers behind the movie

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:27.040
played coy about whether or not it would be
a part of the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:31.200
Rumors were abundant that the movie would
feature a cameo from Tom Holland's Spider-Man,

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.160
or even just Holland as Peter Parker.

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:38.180
Considering the reputation that the MCU has
for consistent, crowd-pleasing quality, the

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:40.040
confusion was to Venom's benefit.

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:45.160
Some people still think the movie is part
of the MCU, which, as we know now, it is not.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:47.240
Here's the breakdown of how it all works.

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:51.160
When it comes to film rights, most of the
Marvel Comics characters are owned by Marvel

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:54.620
Studios, with the exception of the characters
related to Spider-Man.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:59.920
According to Variety, Sony holds the rights
to about 900 characters from the Spider-Man

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:01.760
corner of the Marvel Comics universe.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:05.860
So if Sony owns Spider-Man, then why has Peter
Parker been hanging out with the Avengers

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:06.480
so much?

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:10.620
"Kid, you're an Avenger now."

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:16.600
After the disappointing critical and commercial
performance of Sony's Amazing Spider-Man 2,

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:20.920
Sony and Marvel worked out something of a
shared custody agreement for Spider-Man, giving

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:24.660
Marvel creative rights to the character for
a stretch of five movies.

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:30.160
Those movies include Captain America: Civil
War, Spider-Man: Homecoming, Avengers: Infinity

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:33.440
War, Avengers: Endgame, and Spider-Man: Far
From Home.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:38.030
As part of the agreement, Sony apparently
gave up the right to produce their own live-action

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:39.260
Spider-Man movies.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:43.920
But when Far From Home comes out, that deal
will be done for, and Sony could then potentially

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.680
take Spider-Man back for themselves.

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:50.799
Now that Venom has proven to be a massive
financial success, earning more than $850

00:01:50.799 --> 00:01:55.200
million worldwide since its release, it's
increasingly looking like Sony will be taking

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:56.860
Spider-Man off Marvel's hands.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:02.280
In the "Designing Venom" special feature included
in the home release of the Venom movie, co-writer

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:06.840
Jeff Pinkner teased the likely
possibility of a Venom-Spider-Man crossover,

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:11.080
going so far as to assert that Spider-Man
already exists in Venom's universe.

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:15.439
The topic was brought up as a part of a discussion
about fan outrage over the movie's redesign

00:02:15.439 --> 00:02:19.590
of Venom, with the white spider emblem across
the character's chest being removed for the

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:20.920
live-action adaptation.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:25.219
In the comics, Venom gets the spider emblem
thanks to the symbiote first bonding with

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:26.100
Peter Parker.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.359
But in this version of Venom's origin, the
symbiote goes straight to Eddie Brock, with

00:02:30.359 --> 00:02:31.590
no Spider-Man connection.

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:34.780
As a result, Venom's chest in the movie is
bare black.

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:40.420
"In a movie where Spider-Man exists in the
universe, but does not exist in our story,

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:41.700
it would make no sense."

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:46.180
But as Pinkner puts it, that aspect of the
movie Venom's design could change if he and

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:48.300
Spider-Man ever manage to encounter each other.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:54.500
"In a future movie down the line, that a symbiote
and Spider-Man might not share a moment, and

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:58.020
that symbiote might end up with the Spider-Man
emblem splashed across its chest."

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:03.659
Speaking to Screen Rant before Venom's release,
director Ruben Fleischer characterized a Spider-Man

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:07.240
and Venom crossover as being a sure thing,
eventually.

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:11.220
He also said that the Spider-Man they would
want to use for the crossover wouldn't be

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:15.760
a new actor, but instead the current Peter
Parker performer, Tom Holland.

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:16.760
The filmmaker said,

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:20.780
"I think we laid the groundwork in this film
for a pretty fun match-up in the future…

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:24.930
It feels like that's inevitable, and I know
that Tom Holland's excited about it, and I

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:28.900
know that Tom Hardy's excited about it, so
it's just a question of when and where, I

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:29.400
guess.

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:31.520
That seems like the natural evolution."

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:36.339
Sony has been laying track for a Spider-Man
crossover since before Venom was released,

00:03:36.339 --> 00:03:41.029
reportedly toning the movie down for a PG-13
rating in order to make it easier for the

00:03:41.029 --> 00:03:43.660
two characters' stories to match up with each
other.

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:48.200
It remains to be seen how the filmmakers would
go about making a crossover happen now that

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:49.920
Venom's origin story is so different.

00:03:50.269 --> 00:03:54.840
In the comics, the symbiote was rejected by
Peter before latching on to Eddie, who held

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:56.680
his own grudges against Spider-Man.

00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:01.360
Their mutual hatred of Spider-Man made them
a natural nemesis, and it's unclear how a

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:04.000
crossover would reverse engineer that relationship.

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:08.600
There's also the fact that Tom Hardy's Eddie
is a grown man who lives in San Francisco,

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:12.460
and Tom Holland's Peter is a high school student
who lives in New York City.

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:16.620
Also, the Venom in Venom is kind of a good
guy, why would he want to go beat up a high

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:17.380
school kid?

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:21.780
These issues make it harder to imagine how
a team-up movie would actually work, but if

00:04:21.790 --> 00:04:26.450
the suits at Sony want it bad enough, it's
probably going to happen, especially since

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:28.500
their take on Venom made so much money.

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:30.320
Who cares about the story, right?

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:31.480
There's money to be made.

